# Untitled

Catalytic degradation of organic pollutants for water remediation over Ag nanoparticles immobilized on amine-functionalized metal-organic frameworks
Wen-Zhe Xiao, Ling-Ping Xiao \((\boxed{\triangleright\bigtriangledown})\), Yue-Qin Yang, Shang-Ru Zhai, and Run-Cang Sun \((\boxed{\triangleright\bigtriangledown})\)
Liaoning Key Lab of Lignocellulose Chemistry and BioMaterials, Liaoning Collaborative Innovation Center for Lignocellulosic Biorefinery, College of Light Industry and Chemical Engineering, Dalian Polytechnic University, Dalian 116034, China
\(\copyright\) Tsinghua University Press 2022  Received: 8 December 2021 / Revised: 9 March 2022 / Accepted: 13 April 2022
## ABSTRACT
Developing efficient catalysts for organic pollutants degradation is crucial for remediating the current severe water environment, yet remains a great challenge. Herein, we report silver nanoparticles immobilized on an amine-functionalized metal-organic framework (MOFs) \((\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{U i O}\!-\!66\!-\!\mathsf{N H}_{2})\) as a robust catalyst for the reduction of 4-nitrophenol (4-NP). The fabricated \(\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{U i O}\!-\!66\!-\!\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) catalyst exhibits the merits of superior activities (high turnover frequency (TOF) \(3.2\times10^{4}\,\,\mathsf{h}^{-1}\) and \(k\) value \(6.9\times10^{-2}\,\,\mathsf{s}^{-1}\)), cost-effectiveness under the lowest \(\mathsf{N a B H_{4}}\) concentration \((\eta_{\mathsf{N a B H4}}/\eta_{\mathsf{N-P N}}\), 200), outstanding cyclability (10 recycling runs), and observable long-term durability, significantly outperforming previously reported catalytic system. The excellent degradation efficiency is ascribed to the favorable microenvironment modulation of unique MOF structure, which regulates the intrinsic properties of active sites and improves the electron-transfer process. Notably, the \(\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{U i O}\!-\!66\!-\!\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) also promotes the catalytic degradation of several organic pollutants at room temperature and hence could find a broad application for water remediation. This work offers a new avenue for the development of high-performance MOF-based catalysts with excellent activity and durability.
## KEYWORDS
\(\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{U i O}\!-\!66\!-\!\mathsf{N H}_{2}\), catalytic degradation, organic pollutants, 4-nitrophenol, water remediation
## 1 Introduction
Nitroaromatic compounds released in the environment are notorious for being harmful and difficult to be degraded [1, 2]. On the contrary, functionalized anilines, the corresponding reduction products of these organic pollutants, are valuable intermediates and can be used for the production of pesticides, dyes, and pharmaceuticals [3, 4]. Catalytic hydrogenation of nitro-aromatic compounds into novel high value-added organic compounds has been regarded as one of the most effective ways [5, 6]. For instance, 4-nitrophenol (4-NP), which is highly toxic and carcinogenic, has long been considered as a "priority pollutant" [3]. However, the corresponding reduction product of 4-aminophenol (4-AP) displays the great value as a significant material in the synthesis of paracetamol [1, 7–9]. This conversion process is usually assisted with \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) as a reductant. From the perspective of reduction potential (4-NP/4-AP, \(-0.76\,\mathrm{~V~}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}/\mathrm{BH}_{4}\), \(-1.33\,\mathrm{~V~}\)), the reaction is thermodynamically feasible [10]. However, this reaction must be assisted by a catalyst due to the presence of a kinetic hindrance. Remarkably, metal nanoparticles as high-performance catalysts have attracted attention due to their small size and more accessible active sites [11–15]. Among them, Ag nanoparticles are highly preferred because of the extraordinary electronic properties and low cost compared to other precious metals [16–18]. Nonetheless, subject to the limitation of particle distribution and loading capacity, the utilization efficiency of Ag still appears to be unsatisfactory. Therefore, it is necessary to synthesize well-dispersed catalysts that require tunable interactions between the supports and active sites [18].
