The prior reports have overlooked the importance of the supports, which, in fact, play a central role in creating the microenvironment around metal nanoparticles in addition to their intended role (as anchors for dispersed metal nanoparticles) [19–21]. In this field, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have become promising decorating supports due to the tunable structures, high internal surface areas, and uniform pores [22, 23]. Diversified nodes and ligands endow supports with abundant coordination sites that benefit MOFs as ideal hosts for metal nanoparticles [24]. Plenty of successful cases employing MOFs as the substrates to fabricate metal-based nanocomposites at a high temperature have been reported [25, 26]. However, the destroyed MOFs after pyrolysis lost their inherent advantages, thus resulting in a poor interaction between metal nanoparticles and substrates. Notably, the uncoordinated amine groups \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) located at the linkers as the Lewis base (provide lone pair electrons) in amine-functionalized MOFs could efficiently stabilize the transition metal precursors (provide empty d-orbitals) [27, 28]. Moreover, the various functional groups dangling on the linkers could further prevent metal nanoparticles from aggregation via modulating chemical environment of MOFs pore walls, producing enhanced catalysis [29–31]. Accordingly, it is urgent and necessary to
develop novel catalysts with more accessible metal active sites and favorable microenvironment modulation to enhance stability for liquid-phase catalytic reactions.
