## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of UiO-66 and UiO-66-NH2
The UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) were prepared according to the reported method with a minor modification [32]. Typically, \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) \((52\,\mathrm{~mg},\,0.22\,\mathrm{~mmol})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \((36~\mathrm{mg/40~mg},\) \(0.22\,\mathrm{\mmol})\) were dissolved in \(44\,\mathrm{\mL}\) N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{NO})\) containing \(6\,\mathrm{mL}\) acetic acid (HAc, \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{2})\) . The resulting mixture was first sonicated for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) and then heated at \(120~^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Subsequently, after centrifugation and washing with the mixture of methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{O}\right)\) and DMF, the obtained white/yellow precipitate was dried in vacuum at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight.
## 2.2 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Ag}/\mathbf{UiO-66}\) and \(\mathbf{Ag}/\mathbf{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\)
Typically, \(85~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was first dispersed in \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) of fresh \(\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NO}_{3}\) solution \((4\,\mathrm{mM})\) , and followed by a reduction of \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) aqueous solution \((3\,\mathrm{\mM})\) under dark. After centrifugation and washing with deionized (DI) water for three times, the obtained black \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalyst was vacuum dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) overnight. \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) catalyst was prepared by the same method using the support of UiO-66.
## 2.3 Catalytic performance tests of \(\mathbf{Ag}/\mathbf{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\)
The procedure for batch catalytic reduction of 4-NP and organic dyes over \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is described as shown below.
Catalytic activities of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) assisted with \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) as a reductant were evaluated for degradation of six model organic pollutants (4-NP, Rhodamine B (RhB), Rhodamine 6G (R6G), methylene blue (MB), Congo red (CR), and methyl orange (MO)) at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) . Typically, \(15\;\mathrm{mL}\) of 4-NP \(\left(10\;\mathrm{mM}\right)\) was first mixed with \(15~\mathrm{mL}\) freshly prepared \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) \((2.0\,\mathrm{~M})\) aqueous solution. Then \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) of a dispersive catalyst aqueous mixture \((2~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) was added into the yellow solution under a magnetic stirring (800 rpm) at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) . The conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP was measured by ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectrophotometer after the filtration through a \(0.22\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) membrane filter. In the recyclability test, all reactions were carried out according to the procedures above. After each run, the used catalyst was separated by vacuum
filtration, washed with fresh \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) and tested directly in the next run. In the leaching test, the catalyst was separated by vacuum filtration at the reaction of \(30~\mathrm{s}\) , and the filtrate was measured by UV–vis spectrophotometer to determine the conversation of 4-NP to 4-AP.
The procedure for microflow catalytic reduction of 4-NP over \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is described as shown below.
Before the reaction, \(30~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalysts were loaded into a glass column \((5\,\mathrm{\mm}\,\times\,50\,\mathrm{\mm})\) (Fig. S19 in the ESM). Then, an aqueous mixture of 4-NP \((50\;\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) \((200~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) was added into the column with a peristaltic pump (BT101L, Lead Fluid, China) at different flow rates (0.5, 1.5, and \(2.5~\mathrm{mL/min^{-1}})\) at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) . The resulting solution was collected and analysed by a UV–vis spectrophotometer.
## 3 Results and discussion
High-resolution TEM (HRTEM, Fig. 2(c)) showed that the exposed crystal faces of Ag nanoparticles were (200) and (111), and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) further proved that the ultrafine Ag nanoparticles were highly distributed on the MOF (Fig. 2(d)). Moreover, the EDS analysis revealed that the line scan profile for silver agreed well with those for \(Z\mathbf{r}\) and N, which are the main components of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (Fig. 2(e)). These results again demonstrated that the Ag nanoparticles were uniformly distributed in the meso-channels of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\).
In this work, to investigate the catalytic activities of the as
In this work, to investigate the catalytic activities of the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalyst, the 4-NP reduction by \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) was evaluated as a benchmark reaction. The variation of 4-NP was measured through UV–vis spectroscopy after the addition of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) to the alkaline solution. As the reaction processed, the absorption peak of 4-nitrophenolate ion at \(400\ \mathrm{nm}\) decreased along with the increasing new band of 4-AP at \(300\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S7 in the ESM). By analyzing the nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra of the reaction products (Figs. S8 and S9 in the ESM), it further proved that the nitro groups were successfully converted into the corresponding amine groups. As depicted in Fig. 3(a) and movies (Movies S1 and S2 in the ESM), the
completed reduction of 4-NP to 4-AP was achieved within only 90 s immediately after the addition of \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) to the reaction system \((n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}=810)\).
In comparison, the \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66}\) catalyst with no \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups afforded a poor catalytic performance with a complete conversion time of 210 s \((n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}=810)\) (Fig. S10 in the ESM). By applying the pseudo-first-order kinetic model, a good linear correlation between \(\ln(C_{t}/C_{0})\) and reaction time \(t\) could be obtained to evaluate the reaction rates of 4-NP reduction. Based on the detailed experimental condition, a TOF (normalized per mole of \(\mathrm{Ag}\)) as high as \(3.2 \times 10^{4} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) could be derived, which demonstrated superior catalytic performances as compared with that of \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66}\) \((1.4 \times 10^{4} \, \mathrm{h}^{-1}\), Fig. 3(b)). As plotted in Fig. 3(c), the \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) catalyst exhibited an extraordinarily high reaction rate \(k\) of \(6.9 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). In contrast, the estimated reaction rate \(k\) value of \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66}\) catalyst was only \(2.2 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), implying \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) has an enhanced catalytic activity. This result further confirmed that the efficient stabilization/immobilization of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) nanoparticles could be achieved via a strong coordination, which is provided by the lone pair electrons of \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups and empty d-orbital of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) species [15].
For a better understanding of the catalytic capabilities of
\(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\), the influence of different catalyst dosages was carefully investigated and the results are illustrated in Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S11 in the ESM. When the molar ratios of \(n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}\) increased from 540 to 810, the reaction rate \(k\) value was significantly improved from \(2.7 \times 10^{-2}\) to \(6.9 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Further addition of the catalyst led to a slightly higher \(k\) value \((9.0 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1})\). Subsequently, the influence of the \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration on the catalytic activity was evaluated, which exhibited a similar trend to that of catalyst dosages. Impressively, it can be found that the concentration of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) favours the 4-NP reduction with a constant \(k\) value up to \(6.9 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Notably, increasing the \(n_{[\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}]}/n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}\) molar ratio to higher than 200 did not have a critical effect on the catalytic performance. This was also verified by the pseudo-first-order kinetic curves (Fig. 3(e) and Fig. S12 in the ESM). These observations fundamentally implied that the \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration was the vital factor to control the catalytic performance during the liquid-phase catalytic reactions. Accordingly, it can be concluded that the optimal reaction condition for 4-NP reduction was using \(n_{[\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}]}/n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}\) and \(n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]}/n_{[\mathrm{Ag}]}\) molar ratios of 200 and 810, respectively.
Moreover, the \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) did not show the activity for the reduction of 4-NP under the light, which was evidenced by the
control experiment (Fig S13 in the ESM). In addition, the conversion of 4-NP over \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) under light and dark is similar (Fig. S14 in the ESM). Currently, heterogeneous catalysts, including CdS, \(\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4},\) have been reported as emerging green technologies used for photocatalysis reactions [35–38]. These advanced nanostructured materials show promising photocatalytic activity due to their strong quantum size effect and appropriate band positions. However, the electronic conductivity of the pristine MOFs used in the present work was inadequate, indicating there was no photocatalytic activity during the hydrogenation of 4-NP to 4-AP under the reaction conditions [39].
Durability is the essential criterion for the actual application of catalysts [40,41]. The longevity of the developed catalyst was established by two tests. First, they were recycled 10 times and exhibited undiminished activity (Fig. 3(f)). The leaching tests indicated that no further conversion of 4-NP was observed as the solid catalyst was separated from the reaction solution by filtration (Fig. S15 in the ESM). Second, the durability of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalyst under a long-term microflow reduction condition was examined (Fig. 4(a)). When the microflow 4-NP reduction was conducted at \(298\;\mathrm{K}\) at a flow rate of \(0.5\;\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) (contact time of \(8\,\mathrm{~s~}\)), the conversion of 4-NP was maintained at \(100\%\) over \(3\mathrm{~h~}\) (Fig. 4(b)). This result indicated that the chemical microenvironment around Ag nanoparticles could easily modulate the electronic interaction between Ag species and supports, thus enhancing the catalytic durability [42]. Subsequently, the activity of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) gradually decreased, and at \(35\;\mathrm{h}\), the yield eventually fell to \(10\%\). At a flow rate of \(1.5\;\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), a significant decrease in the conversion of 4-NP was observed after \(2\;\mathrm{h}\), and at \(26\;\mathrm{h}\), the conversion had fallen to \(13\%\). Rapid flow, therefore, facilitated the deactivation of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). In the microflow reduction at a flow rate of \(2.5\;\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\), the catalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was almost lost after \(18\,\mathrm{h}\).
ICP analysis of the liquid (flow rate of \(1.5\;\mathrm{mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\)) revealed that the Ag content was 5.1 ppb when 4-NP conversion was \(100\%\) for \(2.5\;\mathrm{h}\). Remarkably, prolonging the test time to \(15\;\mathrm{h}\) with a 4-NP conversion of \(50\%\) resulted in almost unchanged Ag content of \(2.1\;\mathrm{ppb}\), validating the leaching resistance and durability of the supported metal catalyst. Moreover, the valence state of \(\mathrm{Ag}\) was found to be very stable during the reaction as revealed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization (Fig. S16 in the ESM). However, HRTEM analysis of the recycled \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalyst after the long-term reduction reactions revealed the presence of large aggregates and enlargements of the Ag nanoparticles during the microflow reactions (Fig. S17 in the ESM). Accordingly, the size changes of the nanoparticles may be probably responsible for the deactivation of the catalyst. Anyhow, both batch and flow-through reaction conditions demonstrated the excellent stability and durability of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\).
To more intuitively compare our work with previous studies, we plotted the TOF according to different types of metal catalysts, and \(k\) values, as well as different ratios of \(n_{\mathrm{[NaBH_{4}]}}/n_{\mathrm{[4-NP]}}\) (Fig. 5 and Table S3 in the ESM). Clearly, as described in Fig. 5(a), the performance of \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) for catalytic 4-NP reduction in this present work displays one of the highest TOF and exceeds most of monometallic and bimetallic catalysts (such as Ag [43–48], Pd [49–51], Au [52–56], Co [57, 58], Pd-Ag [59], and Pd-Au [60]). It has a \(k\) value of \(6.9\,\times\,10^{-2}\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), to the best of our knowledge, which is among the top of the state-of-the-art (Fig. 5(b)). Moreover, our catalytic system showed a higher catalytic efficiency, which could be achieved with a relatively lower \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration as compared to the reported data (Fig. 5(c)), thus undoubtedly reducing the cost for organic wastewater treatment. In short, the optimized \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalytic system towards
4-NP reduction exhibits the merits of superior activities (TOF 3.2 \(\times\ 10^{4}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and \(k\) value \(6.9\,\times\,10^{-2}\,\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) , cost-effectiveness under the lowest \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration \((n_{[\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}]}/n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]})\) , 200), and outstanding cyclability (10 recycling runs) as well as good longterm durability, outperforming the capacity of many previously reported catalysts.
Combined with the experimental results and the literature reports [17, 61], the suggested reaction mechanism of the \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) - assisted 4-NP reduction over \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) catalyst is schematically illustrated in Fig. 6. The catalytic reduction process of 4-NP mainly involves the hydrolysis of \(\mathrm{BH_{4}}\); the adsorption of 4-NP, and the desorption of 4-AP. Firstly, the generated active hydrogen after \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) hydrolysis would be transferred to the metal surface. Subsequently, 4-NP adsorbed on Ag nanoparticles further reacted with active hydrogen and was hydrogenated to produce 4-AP. Among them, \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups provide an excellent environment for the stabilization and immobilization of Ag nanoparticles to ensure that Ag nanoparticles could be subtly dispersed on the amine-functionalized MOF structures. In addition, the porous structure of \(\mathrm{UiO}/66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) can promote the diffusion of substrates and products, which greatly accelerates the reaction rate. Accordingly, it can be concluded that the favorable microenvironment modulation of MOF structure regulates the intrinsic properties of active sites and thus improves the electron-transfer process in the \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) -assisted 4-NP reduction system.
The highly active and stable Ag nanoparticles immobilized on \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) encourage us to explore their potential for catalytic reduction of other organic contaminants in wastewater, including cationic dyes (ie, RhB, R6G, and MB) and anionic dyes (ie, CR and MO), which are well-known toxic pollutants to human health [62]. As depicted in Fig. 7, the \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) system exhibited extremely fast removal rates when reacting with RhB and R6G, moderate rates when reacting with CR and MO, and slow reaction rates when reacting with MB. Benefiting from the broad substrate-reactivity and compatibility, the prepared \(\mathrm{Ag}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) offered
significant advantages as an efficient and versatile catalyst towards selective degradation of organic pollutants for water remediation under mild conditions. Such outstanding catalytic performance indicates that the strategy of immobilizing metal nanoparticles on MOF paves an effective way for developing highly active heterogeneous catalysts.
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a facile approach to fabricate \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) catalyst utilizing ammine-containing \([\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2}]^{+}\)complex as a precursor to be immobilized on UiO-66- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\). The Ag nanoparticles exhibit good dispersibility in \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) due to the stabilizing effect of \(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\) groups. Impressively, the performance of \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) exhibits the merits of superior activities (TOF \(3.2 \times 10^{4}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\) and \(k\) value \(6.9 \times 10^{-2}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)), cost-effectiveness under the lowest \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) concentration \((n_{[\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}]}/n_{[4-\mathrm{NP}]})\), 200), and outstanding cyclability (10 recycling runs). Further experimental studies disclose that the favorable microenvironment modulation of unique MOF structure regulates the intrinsic properties of active sites and improves the electron-transfer process. Additionally, the \(\mathrm{Ag/UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) also promotes the catalytic reduction of several organic pollutants at
room temperature and hence could find a broad application in the areas of water remediation. We envision that the present study can provide an advanced strategy for the rational design of MOF-based catalysts with excellent activity and durability in the sustainable treatment of wastewater pollutants.
